==Reactions==
*'''[[Indonesia]]:''' President [[Megawati Sukarnoputri]] said in a speech on Armed Forces Day, 5 October, "We have to show to the world we are a big nation. All components of the nation have to stick to the agreement we have struck that whoever is elected in the democratic election has to be accepted. We may have chosen differently. However, we had the same objective: that is to bring the nation to a better future."<ref>{{Cite news | last = Moore | first = Matthew | title = Megawati still keeping Yudhoyono on hold | url = http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2004/10/05/1096949513009.html | work = [[The Sydney Morning Herald]]  | date = 6 October 2004 | access-date =20 June 2009}}</ref>
*'''[[Australia]]''': Prime Minister [[John Howard]] said he planned to attend Yudhoyono's inauguration. Howard said, "I look forward to working closely with Dr. Yudhoyono and his new government to continue and extend the close cooperation between our two nations, particularly on counter-terrorism."<ref>{{Cite news | last = Forbes | first = Mark | title = PM to see new leader sworn in | url = http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2004/10/14/1097607368339.html | newspaper = [[The Age]]| date = 15 October 2004 | access-date =20 June 2009}}</ref>
*'''[[People's Republic of China]]''': [[Politburo Standing Committee of the Communist Party of China|Politburo Standing Committee]] member [[Wu Guanzheng]] met with Yudhoyono on 13 October and conveyed congratulations from President [[Hu Jintao]].<ref>{{Cite news | title = Senior Chinese leader meets Indonesia's president-elect | url = http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2004-10/15/content_2091733.htm | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20121024135025/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2004-10/15/content_2091733.htm | url-status = dead | archive-date = 24 October 2012 | agency = [[Xinhua News Agency]] | date = 14 October 2004 | access-date =20 June 2009}}</ref>

==Aftermath==
{{Further|First inauguration of Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono}}
[[Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono]] and [[Jusuf Kalla]] were sworn in as president and vice president on 20 October. It was the first Indonesian presidential inauguration to be attended by foreign leaders.<ref>{{Harvnb|Ananta|Arifin|Suryadinata|2005|p=121|Ref=none}}</ref> After a selection process that began on 15 October, the [[United Indonesia Cabinet]] was announced later following the inauguration. The 36-member cabinet was sworn in at the [[Istana Negara, Jakarta|State Palace]] on 21 October.<ref>{{Harvnb|Ananta|Arifin|Suryadinata|2005|p=128|Ref=none}}</ref> The process of selecting candidates for Yudhoyono's cabinet was considered "decent, intelligent, and indecisive". While attempting to satisfy as many members as possible in his coalition of small parties, Yudhoyono created a cabinet that was neither quite as cohesive nor effective. However, Kalla soon became the party chairman of Golkar in late 2004 and turned the party's votes in the DPR to those in favour of the government, creating an effective ''bloc'' and leaving Megawati's PDI–P as the largest non-government party in the DPR.<ref>{{Harvnb|Ricklefs|2008|pp=404–405|Ref=none}}</ref> Although the first one hundred days of Yudhoyono's administration saw continuing economic stability, it was overshadowed by the events of the [[2004 Indian Ocean earthquake|Indian Ocean earthquake]] which occurred on 26 December. The earthquake triggered [[tsunami]]s that destroyed the coastline of the provinces of [[Aceh]] and [[North Sumatra]] and killed more than 200,000 people.<ref>{{Harvnb|Ananta|Arifin|Suryadinata|2005|pp=132–133|Ref=none}}</ref>

